WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.291 --> 00:00:04.921
- How do you want the everyday basketball
fan to view the WNBA? 

00:00:04.921 --> 00:00:05.755
- Badass.

00:00:05.755 --> 00:00:06.715
Right?

00:00:07.173 --> 00:00:10.051
Do you have to say that LeBron is a 
"male basketball player"?

00:00:10.051 --> 00:00:12.095
Like, I don't understand why 
we have to do that.

00:00:12.595 --> 00:00:14.597
It felt like so much of 
my life was hidden

00:00:14.597 --> 00:00:16.433
from even members of 
my family.

00:00:22.814 --> 00:00:27.110
- I'm on my way to D.C. to link up with
Washington Mystics forward Elena Delle Donne.

00:00:27.944 --> 00:00:31.573
Bouncing back from an injury, 
she has a lot to prove this season.

00:00:31.906 --> 00:00:36.619
But she's already proving why more people
need to take notice of the WNBA.

00:00:40.957 --> 00:00:44.002
- Hello.
- Hi, it is so good to see you!

00:00:44.002 --> 00:00:45.253
- What's up, how are you?

00:00:45.253 --> 00:00:46.838
- Long time, no see.
- I know.

00:00:47.464 --> 00:00:49.340
We gotta do, like, a side by side, gosh.

00:00:49.340 --> 00:00:51.593
- We're old now.
- Yeah, what happened to us?

00:00:54.679 --> 00:00:57.307
- [Announcer]: 19-for-19,

00:00:57.307 --> 00:01:00.060
and no one has been better
in the history of the league.

00:01:03.855 --> 00:01:10.153
- You have been called
the best free-throw shooter in pro basketball.

00:01:10.153 --> 00:01:13.406
Are you the best free-throw shooter
in pro basketball?

00:01:13.406 --> 00:01:14.991
- I don't know.
- [Laughing]

00:01:14.991 --> 00:01:18.870
- I mean, maybe. But, um–
- I think you know the answer.

00:01:18.870 --> 00:01:22.332
- It's not, like–
it's not a title I would, I like, take.

00:01:22.332 --> 00:01:27.378
I just know that I have kinda just 
figured out what works for me

00:01:27.378 --> 00:01:31.966
and the simplicty of the shot
and not letting my mind get too involved

00:01:31.966 --> 00:01:33.802
cuz free-throw shooting is
so mental.

00:01:33.802 --> 00:01:36.429
- So what does work for you
when it comes to shooting free throws?

00:01:36.429 --> 00:01:39.224
- Making it the same and 
keeping it very simple.

00:01:39.224 --> 00:01:43.144
So, like, I literally get a 
90 degree angle with my arm

00:01:43.144 --> 00:01:44.896
and then lift and flick.

00:01:44.896 --> 00:01:46.314
And that's really it.

00:01:46.314 --> 00:01:48.858
You'll see players, like, 
get up there and

00:01:48.858 --> 00:01:52.278
spin the ball, or, like, put it behind their back, 
or between their legs,

00:01:52.278 --> 00:01:54.823
and when you do that, 
so much can go wrong.

00:01:54.823 --> 00:01:57.575
Like, you will be thinking about that
when you take your shot,

00:01:57.575 --> 00:01:58.535
and that's just your routine.

00:01:58.535 --> 00:02:00.537
Like, why should your routine
affect the shot?

00:02:00.537 --> 00:02:02.747
- [Announcer]: Just—
- Whoa. Oh, whoa.

00:02:02.747 --> 00:02:05.208
- It was wide right.
- Oh, whoa. Oh my goodness.

00:02:05.750 --> 00:02:12.132
- Which do you prefer to be described as, 
"basketball player" or "female basketball player"?

00:02:12.132 --> 00:02:13.591
- Basketball player.

00:02:13.591 --> 00:02:16.761
Do you have to say that LeBron is
a "male basketball player"?

00:02:16.761 --> 00:02:18.429
Like, I don't understand
why we have to do that.

00:02:18.429 --> 00:02:20.765
Like, we're basketball players. 
We play basketball.

00:02:21.224 --> 00:02:25.770
- How do you want the everyday
basketball fan to view the WNBA?

00:02:25.770 --> 00:02:27.522
- Badass.
Right?

00:02:27.522 --> 00:02:30.191
- Badass basketball.
- Badass basketball.

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:36.447
- How does the everyday basketball fan
view the WNBA?

00:02:37.615 --> 00:02:43.580
- I think the true fan, or someone who has
played basketball and truly knows the game,

00:02:43.580 --> 00:02:44.706
loves it.

00:02:44.706 --> 00:02:46.499
I mean, you hear the NBA guys
talk about it.

00:02:46.499 --> 00:02:50.753
They watch our games. They study our games
to learn stuff from us.

00:02:50.753 --> 00:02:55.049
It's those high school has-beens
that think they can still play

00:02:55.049 --> 00:02:58.887
and for some reason hate that females might be
better than them at something.

00:02:58.887 --> 00:03:02.432
They're the ones who 
struggle to jump on board.

00:03:02.432 --> 00:03:06.102
- [Announcer]: Natasha Cloud, going right, going left.
At the horn!

00:03:06.102 --> 00:03:08.313
- [Cheering]

00:03:08.313 --> 00:03:12.859
- When someone says that women's basketball
is fundamental basketball–

00:03:12.859 --> 00:03:15.153
- Mhm.
- How do you view that term?

00:03:15.153 --> 00:03:18.323
- Fundamental, that word just
is a little boring.

00:03:18.323 --> 00:03:21.993
It's almost like they're kinda just 
simplifying our game and saying, like,

00:03:21.993 --> 00:03:25.914
"It's very fundamental" and, you know,
"It's way different than the NBA."

00:03:25.914 --> 00:03:28.333
Like, we can't just jump over 
people and dunk.

00:03:28.333 --> 00:03:31.961
Like, a lot of times we have to get
to the rim and get real creative

00:03:31.961 --> 00:03:35.173
and all different finishes,
so the game is different.

00:03:35.173 --> 00:03:38.301
But I don't think there needs to be 
that comparison all the time.

00:03:38.301 --> 00:03:43.431
And even just the small little naysayers
who are constantly, like, on us,

00:03:43.431 --> 00:03:45.642
like "Go back to the kitchen, 
go back to the kitchen."

00:03:45.642 --> 00:03:47.227
- What is it with the kitchen?

00:03:47.227 --> 00:03:49.229
- Like, what is the kitchen comment?
- What time are we living in?

00:03:49.229 --> 00:03:52.440
- Cuz I can't cook. Yeah.
- And I can't either.

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:55.735
I mean, at least say, 
"Order Postmates" or something.

00:03:55.735 --> 00:03:57.445
- Right, more accurate.
- Yeah.

00:03:59.614 --> 00:04:02.450
- You talked about the marketing of WNBA players,

00:04:02.450 --> 00:04:05.912
and I know that last year you called out
Adam Silver for not investing 

00:04:05.912 --> 00:04:08.456
enough marketing dollars
into the game.

00:04:08.456 --> 00:04:12.418
- You can't make a business plan
20 years ago and keep it the same for 20 years.

00:04:12.418 --> 00:04:15.088
You know, it's gotta change as
the times have changed.

00:04:15.088 --> 00:04:18.716
And now is the time where, you know, women
are at the forefront of so much,

00:04:18.716 --> 00:04:21.094
and women are so powerful and motivational.

00:04:21.094 --> 00:04:25.765
So I think the W can be something that
women can look to for that inspiration.

00:04:28.559 --> 00:04:32.480
- There's this idea, though, that if we
talk about anything that has to do with a woman,

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:35.566
we almost do it in comparison to a man.

00:04:35.566 --> 00:04:38.069
- That's, like, something I've
always struggled with.

00:04:38.069 --> 00:04:41.447
There's been so many NBA guys talking
about the WNBA, and it's

00:04:41.447 --> 00:04:44.242
frustrating that they have to talk
about it for us to be legit.

00:04:44.242 --> 00:04:48.162
And people do listen when they talk, and
unfortunately, people aren't listening as much

00:04:48.162 --> 00:04:50.623
as when we talk.
Why can't we just be heard?

00:04:50.623 --> 00:04:53.668
- What NBA players really do
support the league?

00:04:53.668 --> 00:04:54.627
- I mean, LeBron,

00:04:54.627 --> 00:04:58.256
even during our playoffs. 
Like, I was able to meet up with him

00:04:58.256 --> 00:04:59.632
cuz I was doing the barbershop.

00:04:59.632 --> 00:05:03.261
He was just, like, spitting out stats
and all this stuff,

00:05:03.261 --> 00:05:07.181
Like, "In this game, at this minute,
why did this happen? You need to do this.

00:05:07.181 --> 00:05:09.309
You need to speak out. 
Like, it was great.

00:05:09.309 --> 00:05:12.520
And it was just, like, 
normal basketball talk.

00:05:12.520 --> 00:05:14.647
And I was like, "OK." 
Like, you truly watch. 

00:05:19.527 --> 00:05:20.903
- [Crowd] Demand excellence! 

00:05:21.654 --> 00:05:25.116
- What does gender equity
look like in the WNBA? 

00:05:25.116 --> 00:05:27.201
- We're not asking for NBA money.

00:05:27.201 --> 00:05:31.331
We know that our league is not generating
the dollars that the NBA is.

00:05:31.331 --> 00:05:35.126
But we do deserve more
than we get.

00:05:35.126 --> 00:05:39.881
The more and more we get marketing dollars
behind it, we get to be on TV more.

00:05:39.881 --> 00:05:43.259
That's when we can continue to grow and
salaries will continue to grow.

00:05:43.926 --> 00:05:45.720
- So for someone who says, 

00:05:45.720 --> 00:05:49.724
"OK, well, maybe you guys don't
get enough because not enough people watch,"

00:05:49.724 --> 00:05:51.309
what would your response
be to that?

00:05:51.309 --> 00:05:54.228
- The WNBA playoffs were on
ESPNews.

00:05:54.228 --> 00:05:57.523
So, like, who's really able to watch?

00:05:57.523 --> 00:06:01.569
Like, I've always said the WNBA fans
are probably the most loyal fans there are

00:06:01.569 --> 00:06:07.033
because, you have to be able to sign up for
League Pass to be able to watch most of the games

00:06:07.033 --> 00:06:10.036
or follow your favorite team. 
Or, like, say you wanna buy a hat.

00:06:10.036 --> 00:06:12.914
A lot–a lotta times there's one
hat you can choose from.

00:06:12.914 --> 00:06:15.708
Like, you can't rock
really cool hats.

00:06:15.708 --> 00:06:18.127
- That has to be so frustrating.
Cuz even when you talk about it, you're like,

00:06:18.127 --> 00:06:19.754
"And they can't– They don't even
have hats!"

00:06:19.754 --> 00:06:21.923
- Yeah, you can't even get a hat!
- Yeah.

00:06:21.923 --> 00:06:24.801
Or like, they can't even get 
a kid's size jersey at times.

00:06:24.801 --> 00:06:29.305
That's the stuff that's frustrating.
But I have seen a little bit more of a shift

00:06:29.305 --> 00:06:33.518
with the Women's World Cup coming up. Like there's
a lot that is about to happen this summer.

00:06:33.518 --> 00:06:36.562
- How did it feel for you to see, like, 
all the Women's World Cup stuff and

00:06:36.562 --> 00:06:39.982
the soccer players saying,
"OK, we're gonna take a stand against this."?

00:06:39.982 --> 00:06:42.819
- It's inspiring to everybody 
else looking up to them,

00:06:42.819 --> 00:06:46.364
and seeing, like, "You know, this is wrong."
Like, "Let's fix it."

00:06:46.364 --> 00:06:50.451
They just had the game where they wore
a female that inspires them.

00:06:50.451 --> 00:06:52.495
Beyonce was on a jersey.

00:06:52.495 --> 00:06:54.247
Like, Lorde was on a jersey,

00:06:54.247 --> 00:06:58.126
and then there was Delle Donne. 
I was like, "Wow." Like, "This is pretty cool."

00:06:58.126 --> 00:07:01.045
- If you were to wear a jersey, 
whose name would you put on the back?

00:07:02.046 --> 00:07:03.047
- I think Pink.

00:07:03.047 --> 00:07:06.592
She's just constantly spoke her mind
and done what's right,

00:07:06.592 --> 00:07:09.762
and even if it has hurt her at times,
like, she didn't care.

00:07:09.762 --> 00:07:11.639
She always just kinda
did the right thing, and

00:07:11.639 --> 00:07:14.809
she's just so powerful and
so important to women.

00:07:15.768 --> 00:07:17.103
- So you live in D.C.

00:07:17.103 --> 00:07:19.647
You can't really get away from 
the politics being here.

00:07:19.647 --> 00:07:26.237
Has being in this environment forced you to be less
silent about political things that you feel matter?

00:07:26.237 --> 00:07:32.201
- I think just what's going on in politics right now
has forced me to really try to speak up.

00:07:32.201 --> 00:07:35.997
Who we have in office is a little 
frightening at the moment. What's going on

00:07:35.997 --> 00:07:40.793
and just, I don't know, the hatred
that is being brought up is scary to me.

00:07:40.793 --> 00:07:43.212
- Yeah.
- We've got to speak up, and I know it gets tiring

00:07:43.212 --> 00:07:48.009
every day something new to kind of say
"No, stop. This is wrong."

00:07:48.009 --> 00:07:50.178
But we kinda just gotta keep pushing.

00:07:50.178 --> 00:07:52.054
- You've been in the news a 
couple times.

00:07:52.054 --> 00:07:55.558
You had a disagreement with Adam Silver.
You wrote a book. 

00:07:55.558 --> 00:07:58.352
What inspired you to just be
more vocal?

00:07:58.352 --> 00:08:00.855
- Truly, just meeting Amanda, 
my wife,

00:08:00.855 --> 00:08:06.194
made me feel at home and she's just
helped me grow up and kinda just be who I am

00:08:06.194 --> 00:08:08.362
and not be apologetic about it.

00:08:08.362 --> 00:08:11.741
When you first meet her, she like, 
comes off as, like, very serious.

00:08:11.741 --> 00:08:14.827
Sometimes she'll have–
I don't know if I should say this,

00:08:14.827 --> 00:08:17.246
but, like, that resting bitch face.
- [Laughing]

00:08:17.246 --> 00:08:19.040
You can say it.
- OK, the resting bitch face.

00:08:19.040 --> 00:08:19.499
- Yeah.

00:08:19.499 --> 00:08:23.377
- But she truly has one of the 
biggest hearts I've ever seen.

00:08:23.377 --> 00:08:26.964
And for those she cares about,
she goes above and beyond.

00:08:26.964 --> 00:08:31.177
Like, she runs all my businesses.
She's doing the renovations.

00:08:31.177 --> 00:08:36.182
- So tell me what that process has been like.
- Whew, that's been, uh, quite fun so...

00:08:36.182 --> 00:08:42.063
We decided to buy this house, and we
knew it was gonna take some, like, fixing.

00:08:42.063 --> 00:08:46.692
Basically, Amanda kind of had the vision.
She was all over Pinterest,

00:08:46.692 --> 00:08:48.569
and was like, "Do you like this? 
Do you like this?"

00:08:48.569 --> 00:08:49.529
I was like, "Yes, yes,"

00:08:49.529 --> 00:08:52.365
and worked with the contractor 
to get it done. 

00:08:53.616 --> 00:08:57.119
- Now, Amanda, your wife told me
you are the boss, you're in control.

00:08:57.119 --> 00:09:00.665
So I wanna know what it's been like to
lose control of your house for all these months.

00:09:00.665 --> 00:09:03.751
- Terrible.
We're living in our basement with our two dogs,

00:09:03.751 --> 00:09:06.546
and they're not small dogs, so
there's that.

00:09:06.546 --> 00:09:11.509
And I like to think you put in some–
- I said you would show me Pinterest, and then

00:09:11.509 --> 00:09:12.927
I'd say yes or no.

00:09:12.927 --> 00:09:15.721
- That was the process.
- Yeah, it’s that simple. Yes or no.

00:09:15.721 --> 00:09:16.681
- It’s simple!
- [Laughing]

00:09:16.681 --> 00:09:18.641
- It was really amazing to hear 
Elena talk about you–

00:09:18.641 --> 00:09:21.269
- I said I was the goofy one and you're
the dry humor one.

00:09:21.269 --> 00:09:24.730
- Yeah. You're weird.
It's not a bad thing. I think it's great to be weird.

00:09:24.730 --> 00:09:28.526
Actually, people don't really know her.
I think they've gotten to know you better

00:09:28.526 --> 00:09:31.779
through Instagram. 
Because I love just recording her

00:09:31.779 --> 00:09:33.531
so people can see what she's really like
cuz she's–

00:09:34.991 --> 00:09:36.951
I think you're funnier,
for sure.

00:09:36.951 --> 00:09:38.619
- Thank you. 
I will take that one.

00:09:42.290 --> 00:09:48.337
Growing up and being a lesbian and
not really understanding what was going on,

00:09:48.337 --> 00:09:53.384
not really having a role model to look to and 
just feeling like I had to keep that hidden

00:09:53.384 --> 00:09:54.969
was really tough.

00:09:54.969 --> 00:09:59.098
It felt like so much of my life was hidden
from even members of my family.

00:09:59.098 --> 00:10:02.101
And then when I met Amanda, 
it just all kind of changed.

00:10:02.101 --> 00:10:05.438
- Cheers to the Delle Donne's!
- [Cheering]

00:10:07.231 --> 00:10:09.567
- We do a segment with every guest.

00:10:09.567 --> 00:10:11.402
I'm gonna tell you about a time 
or a moment,

00:10:11.402 --> 00:10:14.447
and you're gonna take me to 
that time or moment.

00:10:14.447 --> 00:10:15.990
- OK.
- Alrighty.

00:10:15.990 --> 00:10:19.410
Take me to when you met your dog.

00:10:20.036 --> 00:10:24.040
- Oh, I was in Chicago, and the
breeder brought him.

00:10:24.040 --> 00:10:26.751
He got out of the little car
and he's, like, floppin' around.

00:10:26.751 --> 00:10:28.794
His ears were huge,
His paws were huge.

00:10:28.794 --> 00:10:30.171
It was love at first sight.

00:10:30.171 --> 00:10:37.553
- [Baby-talking] What you doin’? Come here. 
Come here. Come here! You a good boy!

00:10:37.553 --> 00:10:41.098
- Take me to your TV
guilty pleasure.

00:10:41.098 --> 00:10:46.270
- OK, so I love "How to Get Away with Murder."
But then I love MTV "The Challenge."

00:10:46.270 --> 00:10:49.482
So I think that's definitely my
biggest guilty pleasure.

00:10:49.482 --> 00:10:51.567
- You're a Shonda Rhimes fan, cuz when 
we first met, we were talking about

00:10:51.567 --> 00:10:53.235
how much we love "Scandal." 
- Exactly.

00:10:53.235 --> 00:10:56.656
- Yeah.
- Anything Shonda's got out, I'm for it.

00:10:57.114 --> 00:11:00.951
- Take me to the time you realized,
"Hey, I'm tall."

00:11:00.951 --> 00:11:04.246
- Oh my goodness.
When I came out of the womb?

00:11:04.246 --> 00:11:05.206
No, I'm just kidding.

00:11:06.707 --> 00:11:11.128
I think when I, like, was in school, 
and we started doing, like, school plays.

00:11:11.128 --> 00:11:13.005
You know how you do, like,
one a year?

00:11:13.005 --> 00:11:18.177
And, like, I was always in the back row for
singing parts with, like, all the tall boys,

00:11:18.177 --> 00:11:19.136
and then me.

00:11:19.136 --> 00:11:22.640
And then even, like, when we
could watch the videos back

00:11:22.640 --> 00:11:25.393
and, like, watch what we were
doing in the dances, I was like,

00:11:25.393 --> 00:11:26.894
"My goodness, I am really tall."

00:11:27.937 --> 00:11:31.899
- Now you mentioned some accommodations
with the house because you are a tall person.

00:11:31.899 --> 00:11:34.276
This light?
Tell me about the light.

00:11:34.276 --> 00:11:37.738
- So this used to be, like, a 
drop-down light,

00:11:37.738 --> 00:11:41.492
And, like, right when I first came 
into the house it was like, “Pew.”

00:11:41.492 --> 00:11:46.372
I would've knocked myself out so, I'm not gonna
get concussed walking into my home anymore.

00:11:46.372 --> 00:11:47.373
- Perfect.

00:11:47.373 --> 00:11:50.000
Lots of stuff's happening in here, 
We've got paint.

00:11:50.501 --> 00:11:52.920
Did you guys ever think about
doing the painting yourself?

00:11:52.920 --> 00:11:55.715
- It's simple, but its a headache, 
and it's tedious.

00:11:55.715 --> 00:11:56.215
- Yeah.

00:11:56.215 --> 00:12:00.678
- When you paint yourself, you see
all of the things that, like, you didn't do perfectly.

00:12:00.678 --> 00:12:02.054
- Mhm.
- Where, like, I don't see that,

00:12:02.054 --> 00:12:06.142
and I'm sure they paint better than I do.
- Yeah, I think we can leave it to the professionals.

00:12:06.142 --> 00:12:07.727
- Yeah, just let them do that.

00:12:08.060 --> 00:12:10.938
- So now that you feel
you have found your voice,

00:12:10.938 --> 00:12:13.065
- Mhm.
- What do you want people to hear?

00:12:13.899 --> 00:12:17.653
- I want females to be empowered
and to be strong.

00:12:17.653 --> 00:12:21.866
And if I can say something to inspire, 
you know, the next coming up,

00:12:21.866 --> 00:12:23.492
then, I've done my job.

00:12:23.492 --> 00:12:27.788
And if I just, you know, kept going the way
I first started the game

00:12:27.788 --> 00:12:30.624
where I was just, like, 
just "Elena the basketball player:

00:12:30.624 --> 00:12:33.669
I don't have opinions,"
that wasn't doing me any good and

00:12:33.669 --> 00:12:35.087
it wasn't doing anyone else good.

00:12:35.087 --> 00:12:38.340
So I wanna leave this game better 
than I came into it

00:12:38.340 --> 00:12:43.679
and hopefully inspire the young ones
to speak their mind and keep pushing.

